From: Flax Advisors <support@flaxadvisors.com>
Date: Sun, 17 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hostiface: serve the real OEM InventoryData response

Rewrites the InventoryData GET against the response captured off the OEM chip
on 2026-08-17, and teaches the POST receiver to normalize an OEM push into the
collection layout the translator already reads.

THE BUG THAT COST THREE HOST CRASHES: every earlier version of this handler
modelled the response on protocol-reverse/inventory.json -- but that file is
the BIOS's POST *payload*, not the BMC's GET *response*.  We were replying with
the shape of the request, and the BIOS's RfInventory driver walked a document
with none of the fields it expected and #GP'd at a constant RIP.  The real
response is a normal Redfish document (@odata.*, BiosStaticFiles, Chassis, DRE,
NetworkDeviceFunctions, Registries, SecureBoot, System) whose group CRCs live
at System.Oem.Ami.Inventory.Crc.GroupCrcList as an ARRAY of one-key objects,
not the flat object the payload uses.  Reference:
/vagrant/oem-reference/live-2026-08-17/hi-InventoryData.json

Two deliberate divergences from the OEM body, both following the rule this file
already learned the hard way -- never advertise something you do not serve.
SecureBoot is omitted, because the OEM advertises #SecureBoot.ResetKeys and its
BIOS then GETs that action target; we cannot reproduce that 154-byte response,
and the previous code advertised it anyway pointing at a route we do not have.
DRE and NetworkDeviceFunctions are present but empty, since an empty array is
dereferenceable while a missing key may not be.

The kill switch is INVERTED to an explicit enable.  It used to be a "disable"
file that had to be present to be safe, which meant an rwfs erase silently
re-armed the dangerous path; absent the enable file we now answer 405, which is
the behaviour proven to boot.  A companion flag allows the GET from the
management LAN so the generated body can be diffed against the captured
reference without booting the host.

BiosStaticFiles now carries a real CRC32 per stored asset.  That is what stops
the BIOS re-uploading all seven files every boot: it compares and uploads only
what changed.  Being wrong here merely costs a re-upload; being absent is what
is expensive.

GroupCrcList is remembered, never computed.  The BIOS ships it in its push and
the BMC's only job is to echo it back -- established from the capture, and it
retires the idea that we had to reverse a CRC algorithm.  Zeros mean we hold
nothing, which reads as stale for every group and asks for a full push.

The normalizer merges per group and never replaces wholesale: a steady-state
boot sends an 850-byte delta with no Memory, Processors or PCIe at all, so
wiping a group because the payload omits it would erase the inventory on the
first no-change boot.  Per-item schemas are byte-identical between the OEM
payload and the generic-Redfish fallback, so flax-hi-inventory publishes this
with no changes.

This route stays disabled by default.  Enabling it is a deliberate act and
still wants the offline body diff first.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [platform integration]
